# Erdos Problem #99 - eliminate loaded flat centers from the boundary burden

**Date:** 2026-09-14
**Target:** C-II, canonical contact-minimal full-core Type-II branch
**Dependencies:** double-unit-corner / flat-center overlap; loaded-flat-center singleton unloading; certified minimax phase-star atomic gap `g=0.002123`
**Claim class:** proof-only structural composition awaiting independent audit
**External literature:** no new import

## LEVEL I STATUS

`STILL OPEN`

The double-unit-corner theorem replaces abstract loaded degree slack by three actual
source objects: outer two-edge teeth, loaded flat-pentagon centers, and strongly inward
loaded-to-unloaded unit contacts. The loaded-flat-center term is not a free escape.
Every loaded flat center consumes two singleton stopped trains, while the minimax
`g=0.002123` theorem gives a matching lower bound on the non-singleton rhombic train
population. Subtracting this finite capacity eliminates loaded flat centers from the
boundary burden.

Put

\[
\Delta_2={\pi\over2\sqrt3},\qquad s:=\sqrt{\Delta_2},\qquad g:=0.002123.
\]

On a convergent Type-II subsequence write

\[
t:=\lim{q-k\over D},\qquad
\delta:=\lim{b-P_F\over D},\qquad
\kappa:=\lim{k\over D},
\]

and assume the true force-hull residual satisfies

\[
R_F=o(D).
\tag{BE.1}
\]

Let

- `N_2` be the genuine two-edge outer-tooth count;
- `L_flat^bd` be the loaded flat-pentagon centers produced by the double-unit-corner
  boundary theorem; and
- `E_in` be its distinct strongly inward loaded-to-unloaded unit contacts.

Then

\[
\boxed{
\liminf {N_2+|E_{in}|\over D}
\ge
B_g(t,\delta,\kappa)
:=6\pi+7s+{7g\over2}-14t+3\delta+{7\kappa\over2}.
}
\tag{BE.2}
\]

In particular, after dropping the nonnegative detour/topology bonuses,

\[
\boxed{
\liminf {N_2+|E_{in}|\over D}
\ge6\pi+7s+{7g\over2}-14t.
}
\tag{BE.3}
\]

The right side is positive throughout

\[
\boxed{
t<t_{BE}:={6\pi+7s+7g/2\over14}
=1.82308400497046\ldots .}
\tag{BE.4}
\]

Thus throughout that interval the loaded-flat-center branch cannot absorb the physical
boundary burden.

## 1. Update the boundary theorem to the current atomic constant

The double-unit-corner / flat-center overlap theorem is algebraic in the all-train
constant

\[
C_g:=2s+g.
\]

Replacing its former `g=0.0021` input by the certified `g=0.002123` theorem changes no
geometric step. Its low-`t` conclusion becomes

\[
\liminf {N_2+|L_{flat}^{bd}|+|E_{in}|\over D}
\ge
5\pi+3C_g-{23\over2}t+2\delta+{7\over2}\kappa.
\tag{BE.5}
\]

Equivalently,

\[
\liminf {N_2+|L_{flat}^{bd}|+|E_{in}|\over D}
\ge
5\pi+6s+3g-{23\over2}t+2\delta+{7\over2}\kappa.
\tag{BE.6}
\]

This inherits exactly the original theorem's bridge, degree-excess, and residual
payments; only the scalar train constant is updated.

## 2. Singleton capacity of all loaded flat centers

Let `L` denote all loaded flat centers, so

\[
L_{flat}^{bd}\subseteq L.
\tag{BE.7}
\]

The loaded-flat-center singleton theorem gives the finite incidence inequality

\[
2|L|\le S,
\tag{BE.8}
\]

where `S` is the singleton stopped-train population. The exact train split is

\[
T=T_R+S.
\tag{BE.9}
\]

The terminal ledger together with `f<=Q` gives

\[
\limsup {T\over D}\le5t-2\pi-2\delta,
\tag{BE.10}
\]

while the rhombic transfer of the `g=0.002123` minimax theorem gives

\[
\liminf {T_R\over D}\ge2s+g.
\tag{BE.11}
\]

Therefore

\[
\limsup {S\over D}
\le5t-2\pi-2\delta-2s-g,
\tag{BE.12}
\]

and hence

\[
\boxed{
\limsup {|L_{flat}^{bd}|\over D}
\le
{5t\over2}-\pi-\delta-s-{g\over2}.
}
\tag{BE.13}
\]

No individual KKT multiplier lower bound is used.

## 3. Subtract the loaded-center escape

Subtract (BE.13) from (BE.6). Since all counts are nonnegative and
`L_flat^bd` is literally one summand on the left of (BE.6), the liminf/limsup
combination gives

\[
\begin{aligned}
\liminf {N_2+|E_{in}|\over D}
&\ge
5\pi+6s+3g-{23\over2}t+2\delta+{7\over2}\kappa\\
&\quad-
\left({5t\over2}-\pi-\delta-s-{g\over2}\right)\\
&=
6\pi+7s+{7g\over2}-14t+3\delta+{7\over2}\kappa.
\end{aligned}
\]

This proves (BE.2).

The key point is structural: the boundary theorem's loaded-flat-center term is paid
from the same singleton population already constrained by the rhombic train floor. It
cannot be counted as an independent escape currency.

## 4. Current endpoint constants

The certified minimax/Section-171 endpoint is

\[
t_{23}={4\over9}(\pi+s)+0.0017327
=1.82124623797944\ldots .
\tag{BE.14}
\]

At `delta=kappa=0`, (BE.2) gives

\[
\boxed{
{N_2+|E_{in}|\over D}
\ge0.0257287378742350\ldots-o(1).
}
\tag{BE.15}
\]

Consequently

\[
\boxed{
{1\over D}\max\{N_2,|E_{in}|\}
\ge0.0128643689371175\ldots-o(1).
}
\tag{BE.16}
\]

Every edge counted by `E_in` is an exact loaded-to-unloaded unit contact with inward
angle-bisector component greater than `sqrt(3)/2`, inherited from the double-unit
corner theorem.

The weak-contact-collapse theorem is simultaneous on the same low-`t`, small-residual
branch. At `t=t_23` it independently gives a distinct set `E_weak` with

\[
{|E_{weak}|\over D}\ge0.0185385189371175\ldots-o(1),
\qquad
\sum_{e\in E_weak}\alpha_e=o(D^2).
\tag{BE.17}
\]

Thus every current-endpoint survivor must simultaneously realize

```text
at least 0.0185385 D distinct weak exact contacts carrying o(D^2) total KKT weight,
and
at least 0.0257287 D total outer-teeth / strongly-inward-contact burden.
```

These populations need not be disjoint, and no disjointness is claimed.

## 5. Frontier effect

The boundary branch has lost its loaded-flat-center escape. The current small-residual
endpoint is forced into two simultaneous mechanical pathologies:

1. a linear family of exact contacts whose total normalized KKT weight is negligible;
2. a positive linear population of either genuine outer two-edge teeth or strongly
   inward loaded-to-unloaded contacts.

This is materially narrower than the previous three-way boundary theorem. It still
is not a contradiction: no theorem currently says a strongly inward contact must have
a uniform stress floor, and outer teeth remain compatible with local geometry.

The next closing target is therefore specific. One must couple the weak-contact family
to the boundary burden, for example by proving that the strongly inward contacts must
carry non-negligible aggregate stress, or by showing that a linear tooth population
forces enough weak/transverse train structure to violate mixed rigidity or the
near-extremal diameter topology.

## 6. Scope audit

- The update from `g=0.0021` to `g=0.002123` is purely algebraic and uses the certified
  minimax theorem's all-train and rhombic-only conclusions.
- `L_flat^bd` is a subset of the loaded flat centers counted by the singleton theorem;
  no identification in the opposite direction is used.
- The subtraction uses liminf of the total boundary burden and limsup of the loaded
  center capacity, which is the safe direction.
- The detour and topology bonuses in (BE.2) are nonnegative and are discarded only for
  the weaker universal form (BE.3).
- No KKT weight floor, exact new contact, exact triangle, or strict same-cardinality
  sub-`D` source is asserted.

C-II and Level I remain open.
